WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Switzerland voted to phase out nuclear power, then imported German coal to keep the lights on. This is Unintended Consequences, episode 93. On the afternoon of December 20, 2019, the Mühleberg nuclear plant on the Aare River west of Bern disconnected from the Swiss grid for the last time. The 373 MW reactor had run since 1972. Its owner, BKW, shut the plant on schedule, and Switzerland's own power stations look

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.960
on paper, a little cleaner by nightfall. That winter, when the sun dropped behind the juror, and electric heat came on across the middle land, a share of the missing megawatts arrived as imports from the north. A measurable share of those imports were generated in German coal and lignite plants. The phase out was designed to take a cautious, climate conscious country off nuclear power. Instead, the shutdown shifted part of Switzerland's winter carbon footprint across the Rhine. The distance

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:18.240
decision did not begin as a crusade against carbon accounting. The decision began as a safety judgment after March 11, 2011, when the Fukushima Daiichi accident forced governments everywhere to look again at aging reactors. Switzerland's fleet was small and well run, but the fleet was also old.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:38.920
Beznau-1 on the Aare in Canton, Argao had been connected in 1969. On May 25, 2011, the Federal Council, with Doris Lutard at the Energy Department, announced that no new nuclear plants would be built. The existing five would not be replaced when those five reached the end of their safe lives.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:49.440
That stands hardened into energy strategy 2050, a package of efficiency targets, support for new renewables, and a legal ban on new nuclear instruction.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:02:03.020
Parliament passed the Revised Energy Act in 2016. On May 21, 2017, with Lutard serving as Federal President, Swiss voters approved the strategy by 58.2%.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:18.720
Those voters were not reckless, and those voters were not naive about speed. Only six months earlier, on November 27, 2016, the same electorate had rejected a Green Party initiative that would have forced a much faster shutdown by 2029.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:29.080
The 2017 YES was the moderate Swiss path. Keep the plants that were already running for as long as the nuclear regulator, ENSI, judged them safe.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:47.580
Refused to build new ones, cover the gap with Alpine Hydro, rooftop solar, efficiency, and the European market. In a country that already generated most of its electricity from Hydro and nuclear, and that prided itself on climate diplomacy, the package looked like prudence rather than rupture.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:03:04.140
Germany was exiting nuclear on a similar timetable. France would always have reactors to spare. The Alps stored water. For a consensus democracy working from 2011's information, energy strategy 2050 was a rational answer to a real fear.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:15.120
The law was sequential, not reckless. And that sequence is where the physics problem starts. The Revised Energy Act entered into force on January 1, 2018.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:29.320
The law did not padlock reactor doors. The Act forbade new builds and let existing units run on a safety-based lifetime, which is why Gozgen, Livestot, and the two Besnell units are still on the grid years later.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:42.320
The first physical subtraction was Muleberg. BKW had already decided in 2013 to close Muleberg at the end of 2019 for a mix of economics, politics, and upgrade costs.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:51.500
The ceremony on the RRA was the moment the strategy became megawatts. Muleberg had been about three terawatt hours of winter-capable output.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:04:01.120
Proponents in the Social Democratic Party, the Greens, and parts of the center argued that efficiency and a renewable build-out would more than replace what retired.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.300
Those proponents said Switzerland would remain an island of clean power in the heart of Europe.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:17.340
Official scenarios from the Swiss Federal Office of Energy sketched a 2050 system, dominated by hydro and new renewables, with imports as a residual, not a foundation.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.540
Skeptics were specific, and the skeptics were not only the plant's home cantons.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:34.120
The Association of Swiss Electricity Companies, along with AXPO, ALPIC, and parts of the Business Federation Economy Suisse, warned about a winter electricity gap, the Stromluke.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:44.840
Swiss demand peaks in the cold months, when heat pumps and resistance heating run, solar output collapses, and Alpine reservoirs are being drawn down rather than filled.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.080
Nuclear had been the quiet winter partner of hydro, firm, domestic, nearly carbon-free.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:57.140
The strategy assumed that the European market would supply whatever winter energy Swiss generators could not.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.600
Energy strategy 2050 did not pair the nuclear ban with a committed build of replacement firm low-carbon capacity.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:13.460
There were no new reservoirs on the scale required. There was no domestic gas with capture. There was no long-duration storage at fleet scale.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.720
And, by design, there were no new reactors.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:25.020
Early years looked calm. Hydro had good years. France was still a reliable exporter. Solar installations began to climb.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.400
The gap was a graph in a winter outlook, not yet a political emergency.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:36.540
Then the market, after dark in January, did what markets do. Physics does not grade annual press releases.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:47.880
Switzerland consumes on the order of 55 to 60 terawatt hours of electricity a year, with nuclear historically supplying roughly a third of generation and hydro most of the rest.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:56.080
Three terawatt hours is not a large number on a European scale, and that output was never supposed to be replaced by a coal plant in Argo.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:03.840
The missing energy was supposed to dissolve into a greener market. The market, in January, after dark, does not work that way.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:15.980
When Swiss reservoirs are falling and rooftop solar is producing near nothing, the next megawatt Switzerland can buy is whatever is on the margin in the coupled grids of Germany, France, Austria and Italy.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:23.780
In many winter evening hours after Muleberg went dark, that marginal plant was not a French reactor or a German wind farm.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.920
The plant on the margin was hard coal or lignite, sometimes gas.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.740
The causal chain is almost mechanical.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.920
Close or withhold firm low carbon supply in a winter peaking alpine system.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.560
Leave the residual to cross-border trade.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:49.980
Watch Germany, Switzerland's largest electrical neighbor, run down its own nuclear fleet while lignite and hard coal remained available to set the price and the carbon content of the last kilowatt hour.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.560
Switzerland's production mix stayed among the cleanest in Europe.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.000
Switzerland's consumption mix did not, not in the hours that mattered.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:14.780
Analysis of cross-border flows and of the residual supply mix, the kind of work grid planners and independent carbon intensity trackers rely on, showed the carbon intensity of Swiss electricity consumption rising during winter peaks as nuclear output fell and imports rose.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:22.920
Electrons at the Lauffenburg and Bosse-Court ether connectors do not carry passports, but the fuel that produced those electrons does.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:32.420
A country that could publish almost spotless generation statistics was, on cold nights, partly powered by the Rhineland and the eastern German lignite districts.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.240
Second order effects stacked on the first.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:44.460
Because international climate inventories are territorial, the extra tons were Germany's problem on paper, and Switzerland's only as an import footnote.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:51.020
Guarantees of origin let suppliers market Swiss hydro even when the physical residual mix was dirtier.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:55.720
Households could feel they had bought clean power while the grid was balancing with coal.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:05.900
The long-stalled electricity agreement with the European Union left Switzerland less fully coupled than its neighbors, which complicated both security and the clean sharing of surplus.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:15.340
Inside the country, the missing winter energy became a planning assumption, more imports, more political anxiety, and eventually more fossil backup.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:19.860
Third order effects arrived with the weather and with other people's accidents.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:21.780
Dry years cut hydro.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:31.800
In 2022, after Russia's invasion of Ukraine, gas prices exploded just as a large fraction of the French nuclear fleet was offline for corrosion repairs.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:40.760
Germany, heading toward the April 15, 2023 closure of its last three reactors, burned more coal to keep its own system upright.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:49.680
Switzerland, which had treated the European market as a clean cushion, discovered that market was a cushion stuffed with whatever fuel the continent still had.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.860
The human texture of that winter was not abstract.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:09:00.260
In RO, Swiss grids operators watched evening wraps that used to be absorbed by Muleberg and the French interconnector.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:09.240
In the Bernie's countryside, the Muleberg site went from employer and taxpayer to a decommissioning project scheduled to last into the 2030s.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:21.080
In German mining regions, lignite plants that climate policy had slated for a managed decline, found themselves, once again, the machines that kept a rich neighbor's heat pumps running after sunset.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:31.240
Swiss officials spoke carefully of supply security. Opposition politicians and utility engineers used a blunter word those critics had been using since 2011.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:32.160
Gap.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:39.580
The irony was not that Swiss voters had wanted more coal. Those voters had wanted less nuclear risk and less carbon.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:48.680
The design treated annual renewable energy as a substitute for firm winter capacity and treated a border as the place where carbon stopped counting.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.060
By late 2022, the Federal Council was no longer talking only about 2050.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:10:00.560
The Federal Council ordered a hydropower reserve, paying operators to hold water back for the darkest weeks.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.460
The government also contracted emergency reserve generation.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:12.500
Gas turbines at beer in Argao, built as insurance against a shortage that the 2017 strategy had not supposed to exist.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:18.060
The reserve was expensive for the hours it might run, and the reserve added a further twist.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:24.720
A nuclear phase out justified in part on climate grounds had summoned fossil backup onto Swiss soil.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:36.040
Solar to the country's credit accelerated sharply after 2022, especially on rooftops and alpine installations, and summer afternoons now produced genuine surplus.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:42.420
Winter evenings did not change their minds. Public opinion and parliamentary arithmetic moved.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:50.940
Albert Roestie, who took over the Energy Department in 2023, opened a political path toward lifting the ban on new nuclear construction.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:58.500
Operators of Beznau, Gozgen, and Leibstad planned for long-term operation under ENSI's safety regime.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:06.500
By 2024 and 2025, Bern was formally revisiting the central legal pillar of energy strategy 2050.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.840
That reconsideration is itself unfinished business, not a fairytale reversal.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:19.860
New reactors, if new reactors come at all, take more than a decade, and the plant still running cannot be wished into being twice.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:34.380
The current state is a country with a cleaner summer, a still tight winter, a nuclear fleet in overtime, a gas reserve nobody wanted, and a more honest conversation about the difference between generating clean electricity and consuming it.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.280
Three principles travel well beyond the RA.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:43.860
First, annual energy targets are not the same product as a kilowatt hour at 7pm in January.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:49.080
A year is a convenient unit for a press release, and a terrible unit for a winter peak.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:57.120
If you retire firm low-carbon capacity before firm low-carbon replacement exists, the market will buy whatever still burns.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.700
The clock and the weather decide which plan answers, not the annual average.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:07.020
Second, emissions follow the marginal plant, not the territorial inventory.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:13.720
A border is not a climate policy, and production-based accounting will always invite leakage that looks like virtue.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:20.340
The tons still enters the atmosphere whether the inventory parks that ton in the next country or the next valley.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:28.160
Third, incentive structures and certificate systems will decouple what people think they bought from what the grid actually used,

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.740
unless physics and bookkeeping are forced to tell the same territory.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.560
A label can travel farther than an electron.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.160
The Swiss were not foolish, the Swiss were sequential.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:43.020
As governments write 2030 and 2035 decarbonization targets,

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:50.100
and as data centers and heat pumps add new winter load from Dublin to Virginia, the same sequence is being written again.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:56.260
New demand that arrives after sunset in the cold months will be served by whatever can still run

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:58.560
when the wind is low and the reservoirs are down.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:04.500
When the clean plant that works in the dark closes, who exactly do you think will be on the margin?

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:08.880
We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking through this one with me.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:14.840
If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you tomorrow with another.

